menu "espOS HTTP server"

    config ESPOS_HTTPD_MAX_BODY
        int "Maximum request body size (bytes)"
        default 16384
        range 1024 262144
        help
            Requests with a larger Content-Length are rejected with 413.

    config ESPOS_HTTPD_STACK_SIZE
        int "Server task stack size (bytes)"
        default 8192
        range 4096 32768

    config ESPOS_HTTPD_TASK_PRIORITY
        int "Server task priority"
        default 5
        range 1 24

    config ESPOS_HTTPD_MAX_URI_HANDLERS
        int "Maximum number of URI handlers"
        default 64
        range 8 128

    config ESPOS_HTTPD_SSE_MAX_CLIENTS
        int "Maximum concurrent event-stream clients"
        default 3
        range 1 8
        help
            GET /api/v1/events keeps a socket open per client. The server's
            socket budget is 4 + this value, clamped to
            CONFIG_LWIP_MAX_SOCKETS - 3 (raise LWIP_MAX_SOCKETS for more).

    config ESPOS_HTTPD_SSE_MAX_CONNECT_CBS
        int "Maximum event-stream connect callbacks"
        default 8
        range 4 16
        help
            Each component that publishes to GET /api/v1/events registers one
            callback here, so a newly connected client gets the current state
            immediately instead of waiting for the next change. espOS itself
            ships six (net, wifi, sk, ota, ble, n2k); a firmware that adds its
            own needs room for those too.

            Running out is silent from the browser's side: the stream opens
            and every other component's snapshot arrives, so the one component
            that missed a slot looks merely idle rather than unregistered.

    config ESPOS_HTTPD_SSE_PING_S
        int "Event-stream keepalive interval (s)"
        default 15
        range 5 120

    config ESPOS_HTTPD_WWW_DIR
        string "Mount point of the UI storage partition"
        default "/www"
        help
            The LittleFS "storage" partition holding the gzipped UI bundle
            is mounted here and served at /.

    config ESPOS_HTTPD_DEBUG_CRASH
        bool "Expose POST /api/v1/system/crash (deliberate panic, for testing core dumps)"
        default n

    config ESPOS_HTTPD_AUTH_REQUIRED
        bool "Refuse protected endpoints until an API key is configured"
        default n
        help
            With an empty httpd.api_key the REST API is open to the network
            (the default, so a device that predates authentication keeps
            working after an update). A product build that must never ship
            open turns this on: protected endpoints then answer
            403 auth_unconfigured until a key is set. Requests from the
            setup portal's network are exempt, which is where the first key
            is set. See docs/security.md.

    config ESPOS_HTTPD_MAX_SESSIONS
        int "Maximum concurrent login sessions"
        default 4
        range 1 16
        help
            Cookie sessions minted by POST /api/v1/auth/login, kept in RAM
            (about 48 bytes each). One more login than this evicts the
            session idle longest; a reboot forgets them all. Bearer requests
            use no session.

    config ESPOS_HTTPD_TLS
        bool "Serve the device's own web UI and API over HTTPS (reserved)"
        default n
        help
            Reserved; not implemented. The device's web server is plain http
            because a TLS server on the smaller targets costs RAM the
            application needs, and a self-signed certificate on a boat LAN
            buys little (docs/security.md). Turning this on changes nothing
            yet; the option exists so a future implementation has a stable
            name.

endmenu
